// ImRenderer<BrnGraphics::LionBlendVertex>::Construct by
// BrnGraphics::Im3dBlend::Construct @0x8229B260, as two program pairs --
//
//     slot 0  "LionBlended"       vertex unk_8200DD58 (0x1A4)  pixel unk_8200DF00 (0x10C)
//     slot 1  "LionBlendedZFade"  vertex unk_8200E010 (0x220)  pixel unk_8200E230 (0x1F8)
//
// -- from the four parallel stack arrays Construct fills before the call
// (`addi r27/r26/r25, r10, unk_8200DD58/DF00/E010 - unk_8200E230` for the
// pointers; `li r27, 0x1A4` / `li r27, 0x10C` / `li r27, 0x220` / `li r10,
// 0x1F8` for the sizes; `li r9, 2` for the program count). Being Xenos
// microcode inside the guest image, nothing in the converted asset tree
// supplies a PC counterpart.
//
// These four arrays are the SAME four programs re-authored for D3D9 from the
// Xenos disassembly (tools/re/x360rd.py to read the packages out of the image,
// tools/assets/shaders/xenos.py to disassemble the microcode,
// tools/assets/shaders/ctab.py to name the constants; the four listings are
// reproduced at the head of tools/assets/shaders/brn_lionblend.fx). Both
// vertex programs are 7 / 12 D3D9 ALU slots against the console's 9 / 13, and
// both pixel programs match the console's slot count exactly (4 and 14). No
// visual oracle was used.
